    body: `<p>On mission Sundays in American churches, the story is always the same. Brave missionaries cross oceans and jungles to bring the gospel to people who have never heard it. The emphasis is on sacrifice, heroism, and the life-altering power of the Christian message. Slides show smiling children. Testimonies recount dramatic conversions. The offering plate circulates with a special appeal for the unreached. It is a stirring narrative. It is also, in its selective telling, a profoundly dishonest one.</p>

<p>The parts we do not tell on mission Sundays are the parts that matter most for understanding why the word "missionary" carries, in much of the non-Western world, connotations not of love but of destruction. The history of Christian missions is inseparable from the history of European colonialism. The gospel traveled in the holds of slave ships, in the baggage trains of conquering armies, in the administrative apparatus of colonial governments that used Christianity as a tool of cultural subjugation. This does not mean that every missionary was a colonialist, or that every conversion was coerced, or that the gospel itself is reducible to its colonial carriers. But it means that any honest reckoning with the history of Christian missions must contend with the entanglement of the gospel with empire — an entanglement that was not incidental but structural, not accidental but theological.</p>

<h2>The Doctrine of Discovery</h2>

<p>In 1452, Pope Nicholas V issued the papal bull <em>Dum Diversas</em>, authorizing King Alfonso V of Portugal to "invade, search out, capture, vanquish, and subdue" all non-Christians, to reduce them to perpetual slavery, and to take their possessions and lands. In 1493, Pope Alexander VI issued <em>Inter Caetera</em>, granting Spain sovereignty over the lands Columbus had "discovered" in the Western Hemisphere. These documents — collectively known as the Doctrine of Discovery — provided the theological foundation for European colonialism. They established the principle that Christian nations had a divinely sanctioned right to claim sovereignty over non-Christian lands and peoples.</p>

<p>The Doctrine of Discovery was not a marginal theological opinion. It was official church teaching, ratified by papal authority and implemented by Christian governments across Europe. It provided the legal and theological basis for the conquest of the Americas, the colonization of Africa and Asia, the displacement and destruction of indigenous peoples on every continent. It was cited in U.S. Supreme Court decisions — notably <em>Johnson v. McIntosh</em> (1823) — as the foundation of American property law. Its effects are not historical curiosities. They are structural features of the world we inhabit.</p>

<p>The theological logic of the Doctrine of Discovery rested on a simple premise: non-Christian peoples were, by virtue of their unbelief, less than fully human and therefore incapable of legitimate sovereignty over their own lands and lives. The gospel was not offered as an invitation but wielded as a justification — a theological mechanism for transferring the wealth, land, and autonomy of non-Christian peoples to Christian ones. The cross was not separate from the conquest. It was the conquest's authorization.</p>

<h2>Residential Schools and Forced Conversion</h2>

<p>The residential school system — implemented in the United States, Canada, Australia, and other settler-colonial states — represents one of the most devastating intersections of Christianity and colonialism. In Canada alone, over 150,000 indigenous children were removed from their families and placed in church-run residential schools between the 1880s and the 1990s. The schools were operated by Catholic, Anglican, United, and Presbyterian churches with government funding and government sanction. Their explicit purpose, in the words of one Canadian official, was to "kill the Indian in the child."</p>

<p>Children were forbidden to speak their languages, practice their spiritual traditions, or maintain contact with their families. They were given Christian names, forced to attend Christian worship, and punished — often severely — for any expression of their indigenous identity. Physical abuse, sexual abuse, malnutrition, and medical neglect were widespread. Thousands of children died in the schools. Many were buried in unmarked graves. The Truth and Reconciliation Commission of Canada, which spent six years documenting the system's history, called it "cultural genocide."</p>

<p>The churches that operated these schools did so in the sincere belief that they were saving souls. This sincerity does not mitigate the harm. It deepens it. The damage was done not by people who cynically exploited Christianity for colonial purposes but by people who genuinely believed that the destruction of indigenous culture was a necessary condition for indigenous salvation. The theology was the problem. A theology that cannot distinguish between conversion and cultural destruction, between the gospel and the erasure of identity, between salvation and assimilation is a theology that has lost its way so completely that its sincerity makes it more dangerous, not less.</p>

<p>The pattern extends far beyond North America. In Australia, the Stolen Generations — Aboriginal children forcibly removed from their families and placed in church-run institutions — suffered similar abuses. In Africa, missionaries frequently served as advance agents for colonial governments, establishing schools and hospitals that, however beneficial in their immediate effects, functioned within a colonial framework that undermined indigenous social structures, political systems, and spiritual traditions. In Latin America, the Spanish <em>encomienda</em> system granted colonizers the right to indigenous labor in exchange for Christian instruction — a system that amounted, in practice, to enslavement under a theological veneer.</p>

<h2>The Indigenous Christian Response</h2>

<p>The relationship between indigenous peoples and Christianity is not, however, a simple story of victimization. Indigenous Christians have, from the beginning, engaged the gospel on their own terms — accepting what resonated with their traditions, rejecting what was merely colonial, and creating forms of Christian faith that are rooted in indigenous soil rather than transplanted from European greenhouses.</p>

<p>Liberation theology — born in Latin America in the 1960s and 1970s — emerged from the experience of colonized peoples who read the Bible and found in it not the justification for their oppression but the resources for their liberation. Gustavo Gutierrez, the Peruvian theologian who gave the movement its name, argued that the God of the Bible is a God who takes the side of the poor, the oppressed, and the marginalized — not the side of their oppressors, however Christian those oppressors may claim to be. The Exodus narrative, the prophetic tradition, the Magnificat of Mary, the Sermon on the Mount — all of these point toward a God whose justice flows downward, toward the bottom, toward the people the powerful have forgotten.</p>

<p>Richard Twiss, a Lakota/Sicangu member of the Rosebud Sioux tribe and a Christian theologian, spent his life articulating an indigenous Christian theology that refused the colonial binary — the assumption that becoming Christian required ceasing to be indigenous. Twiss argued that indigenous cultural forms — drumming, dance, ceremony, artistic expression — could be legitimate vehicles for Christian worship, just as European cultural forms — organ music, Gothic architecture, clerical vestments — had always been. The gospel, he insisted, is translatable into every culture precisely because it belongs to none of them. The colonial error was not the introduction of the gospel to indigenous peoples but the insistence that the gospel could only be received in European packaging.</p>

<p>In Africa, the independent church movement — churches founded and led by Africans, free from European missionary control — has produced some of the most vibrant and creative expressions of Christianity in the world. These churches draw on African cultural resources — communal worship, ancestor awareness, healing practices, oral tradition — in ways that European missionaries would not have sanctioned. They represent not a departure from Christianity but a decolonization of it — a refusal to accept the colonial premise that African culture is incompatible with Christian faith.</p>

<blockquote>The gospel does not require the destruction of culture. It requires the transformation of culture. And the people best equipped to lead that transformation are the people whose culture it is — not the foreigners who arrive with the answers already written.</blockquote>

<h2>How to Do Missions Without Colonialism</h2>

<p>The colonial model of missions was built on a set of assumptions that the church must name and reject. The assumption that the missionary brings God to a place where God is absent — as if the Creator of all things had no presence among the peoples he created before the missionary arrived. The assumption that Western culture is the natural container for the gospel — as if the faith born in Palestine, shaped by Greek philosophy, preserved by Middle Eastern monks, and carried to Europe by North African theologians was inherently European. The assumption that the missionary's role is to teach and the indigenous person's role is to learn — as if the encounter between the gospel and a new culture were a one-way transmission rather than a mutual discovery.</p>

<p>Partnership models of mission begin with a different set of assumptions. They assume that God is already at work in every culture and that the missionary's first task is not to speak but to listen — to discern what God has been doing before the missionary arrived. They assume that the indigenous community is not a passive recipient of the gospel but an active participant in its interpretation and embodiment. They assume that the gospel will take forms in the new culture that the missionary could not have predicted and may not recognize — and that this is not a failure of the mission but its success.</p>

<p>Concrete practices follow from these assumptions. Short-term mission trips, as commonly practiced, should be radically reconceived. The typical short-term trip — in which a group of American teenagers spends a week painting an orphanage, taking selfies with local children, and returning home with a story about how the experience "changed their perspective" — is, at best, a form of spiritual tourism. At worst, it reinforces the colonial dynamic: wealthy outsiders arrive, perform charity, extract emotional benefit, and leave. The local community is not transformed. The visitors are not formed. And a great deal of money that could have funded local workers has been spent on airfare.</p>

<p>Long-term mission work, when it is done well, looks like partnership. It means working under indigenous leadership rather than over it. It means funding local organizations rather than building parallel structures. It means learning the language, the culture, the history, and the theological categories of the community being served — not as a prerequisite for preaching but as a form of respect for the people being encountered. It means being willing to receive as well as give, to learn as well as teach, to be changed as well as to change.</p>

<p>It means, ultimately, doing what the incarnation itself demonstrates: entering another's world on their terms, not your own. God did not send the gospel from a distance. God entered human culture, took on human flesh, spoke a human language, ate human food, lived within the constraints of a particular time and place. The incarnation is the model for all Christian mission: not the imposition of one culture on another but the translation of the gospel into the idiom of the community it encounters.</p>

<p>The church cannot undo the damage of colonial missions. The children who died in residential schools cannot be brought back. The cultures that were destroyed cannot be fully recovered. The trust that was violated cannot be easily rebuilt. But the church can tell the truth about what happened. It can repent of the theological errors that made it possible. It can commit to forms of mission that honor the dignity, agency, and cultural integrity of the communities it serves. And it can listen — genuinely, humbly, without defensiveness — to the voices of those who bear the scars of its history.</p>

<p>That listening is not optional. It is the beginning of the repentance the church owes to the peoples it harmed in Christ's name. And it is the only foundation on which a genuinely Christian practice of mission can be rebuilt — a practice that carries the cross without the sword, that offers the gospel without demanding the surrender of identity, and that trusts the Spirit to do what missionaries, in their colonial confidence, always believed only they could do: bring the good news to life in soil that is not their own.</p>`
